#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092973} :activity/id #uuid "81af9a68-b130-5677-9448-07c433454ec2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048625 :topic/id #uuid "af9f4926-ffe7-585c-be25-f4e24467b430" :topic/math-academy-id 921} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093391 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\qquad -\\pi \\leq x < \\pi\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation contains a transformed cosine function.\n\nWe know that the general solution to the elementary equation\n\n$$\n\\cos{X} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= \\frac{π}{3} + 2nπ, \\mid X|= \\frac{5π}{3} + 2nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is assumed to be any integer.\n\nTo find the general solution of the transformed cosine equation, we replace $X$ with the input of the cosine function, which is\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nin this case. This gives the general solution\n\n$$\nx+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi, \\qquad\\qquad x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}+ 2n\\pi\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above two equations for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2nπ \\mid x|= \\frac{4π}{3} + 2nπ \\\\\nx &= \\frac{4π}{3} + \\frac{6nπ}{3} \\\\\nx &= (2π(2 + 3n))/(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\nn = 0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots\n$$\n\nuntil we have generated all solutions in the domain\n\n$$\n-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi\n$$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$. We reject the solution $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-2\\pi$ and $x=-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$. We reject the solution $-2\\pi$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain\n\n$$\n-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\qquad x_2 = 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca38e435-6d4e-550b-83d2-62b4e80d4eda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2684 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "724852af-67e5-54bd-91c0-766dd3dabaf7" :step/math-academy-id 13025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091991 :question/id #uuid "833de87d-9983-5f8d-94aa-3132606fc15d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3775"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6d31a25-823d-59b2-897c-61a75b747c01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049936}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092779}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Cosine Function Transformed by One Operation"} :step/id #uuid "23e0ef78-2c4c-5cea-9f7b-95ad132ae0f0" :step/math-academy-id 13039 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092975}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092173 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091993 :question/id #uuid "d8397378-838b-5fb1-b3e7-b5c341a17e38" :question/math-academy-id "e-3784"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1286cc9b-2080-5541-88e1-d4d2741ea1e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092840}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Cosine Function Transformed by Multiple Operations"} :step/id #uuid "e4478e20-2cd6-5665-be7e-0489a9f6197c" :step/math-academy-id 13046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092976}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092174 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091997 :question/id #uuid "c672314a-e7f7-5f64-9f59-b49698b76b37" :question/math-academy-id "e-3791"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e880672-e776-5701-8168-730f7012f5b0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186